John:  per your request.

The PE pipe industry has been through significant contraction in the last t=
hree years.  The oil patch crash led to a significant rationalization of in=
dustry capacity with pipe makers closing plants, moving plants, and mothbal=
ling lines.  National Pipe got out of PE pipe altogether selling their extr=
uders.

Recently there has been a strong uptick in PE pipe demand into the face of =
reduced capacity (for the time being).  As a result, lead times have stretc=
hed out significantly for the full line pipe producers.  By full line it is=
 meant from small diameter to large diameter.  There are a class of produce=
rs specializing in small diameter.  Lead times we are hearing are 12 weeks =
plus or minus.

Another effect has been an increase in PE pipe prices however PE resin capa=
city is growing and set to grow more so the longer term future will be for =
PE pipe price decline.

ISCO saw all of this coming and early this year bought up DR13.5 pipe inven=
tory in the Southeast commonly used in Golf for the purpose of serving Golf=
.  This gives them a temporary advantage that may even now be finished sinc=
e eventually this inventory is used up and they are back to the pipe makers=
 with orders for production.  However the tight supplies, long lead times, =
their inventory position does give ISCO an advantage in Golf in general.  T=
hey would have influence over production priorities that was muted and less=
 apparent when the pipe industry was in a state of overcapacity and thus an=
 advantage over the typical distributor.

ISCO's recent reenergizing of their Golf effort on a national basis combine=
d with tight supplies of pipe as well as their theoretical ability and ince=
ntive to serve contractors directly makes them even more of a threat to all=
ied supplying Golf distributors than they were in the first phase of PE ent=
ry.  Today PE is the majority of Golf systems and is accepted.  They don't =
need Golf distribution to get PE into the market.  Their infrastructure is =
set up to serve contractors and even on a local basis given all of their br=
anches.  In fact to serve golf through golf distributors is inefficient for=
 them because it underutilizes their systems.

ISCO's position and new activity will also threaten CMF for the same reason=
s it threatens Golf distribution.  Golf distributors associated with CMF wi=
ll not be any less vulnerable to ISCO and in fact will be more vulnerable b=
ecause their pricing will be relatively high.

In this environment an allied serving Golf distributor's best play is to re=
sist the intrusion of PE pipe by promoting PVC systems or PVC mains and PE =
laterals.  Small diameter PE pipe is available from a different set of pipe=
 producers.  In the long run at least pipe and fittings if not also valves =
will be served direct to contractors by PE distributors.  ISCO could easily=
 end up handling wire and swing joints.  ISCO used to be a Rainbird Golf di=
stributor and currently buys from Lasco.

The last thing a Golf distributor should be doing is inviting a golf consul=
tant into his market that is a PE ideologue.

In the long run the best that can happen for him is that ISCO allows them t=
o take a finder's fee but ISCO doesn't need the Golf distributor and Golf c=
ontractors have ISCO on speed dial.  The only other thing a Golf distributo=
r can do to hold the allied at higher margins is to package with the irriga=
tion equipment and proprietary fitting and valve specs that ISCO doesn't ha=
ve access to.  This has limits and distributors have not demonstrated an ab=
ility to do this consistently and effectively because someone breaks.

In the mean time we can continue to prefer Golf distribution with PE produc=
ts and help them with PE pipe access directly from pipe makers.  This acces=
s to pipe makers will be more tenuous with ISCO going active again.  For th=
e Carolinas Flying W is a first choice for STI.  Performance is another goo=
d candidate but they would require more cultivation.  JM Eagle in Georgia i=
s another potential supplier. WL Plastics has plants in Kentucky and Georgi=
a but have nominally been the CMF source.  However, WL Plastics is a huge s=
upplier to ISCO.

If PVC mains and PE laterals are in play, Silverline and Endot are sources =
for small diameter PE pipe we can help them with.

Ultimately STI's best play is to promote PVC.  In time if full PE systems h=
old sway and Golf distribution policy remains as it has, the next phase of =
Golf distribution will be PE allied sold to the job directly by PE distribu=
tion.  And Golf distributors have exited the allied business.

John Goetz consulting with ISCO again has very little to do with what is go=
ing to happen.  He will have an ancillary effect.  The ISCO wild fire will =
grow through the contractor ranks and their superior pricing and availabili=
ty of commodity PE products.  Goetz may provide an entre to a few consultan=
ts but he is as likely to hurt ISCO as help them in that regard.  He really=
 isn't going to matter in the long term if ISCO is serious about this.

